<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-section-OrderVertical" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Order on vertical transformations</title>

  <p> We will see how to apply more than one vertical transformation on the same graph.</p>
    <p>
        Since vertical dilations, reflections across the <m>x-</m>axis, and vertical shifts act all on <m>y-</m>values, it is natural to wonder if applying them in any order will produce the same final graph. Let's see how these transformations interact using the below graph.
    </p>
    <p>
        We will start with the graph of a function <m>f</m> and will consider the transformation <m>A\cdot f(x)+k</m>. We will work using a two-step procedure as follows
    </p>
    <p>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Set <m>A=2</m> and <m>k=3</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Define <m>p(x)=f(x)+k</m> and <m>q(x)=A\cdot p(x)</m>. The final graph will be the graph of <m>q</m> shown in blue.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Now, define <m>g(x)=A\cdot f(x)</m> and <m>h(x)=g(x)+k</m>. The final graph will be the graph of <m>h</m> show in purple.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </p>
    <p>
        <figure>
    <interactive desmos="eonabxkwkq" width="100%" aspect="2:3"/>
    <caption>Order in vertical transformations</caption>
</figure>

    </p>
    <p>
        As you can see, the graphs obtained for step 2 and step 3 are different. Let's discuss what's happening there. 
    </p>
    <exercises xml:id="exercises-OrderVertical">
        <exercise label="multiple-choice-Orderq">
            <title>Multiple-Choice, Randomized, One Answer</title>
            <idx>vert shift first</idx>
            <statement>
                <p>To obtain the graph of <m>q</m>, we</p>
            </statement>
            <choices randomize="yes">
                <choice>
                    <statement>
                        <p>apply a vertical shift 3 down first, followed by a vertical stretch by a factor of 2</p>
                    </statement>
                    
                </choice>
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement>
                        <p>apply a vertical shift 3 up first, followed by a vertical stretch by a factor of 2</p>
                    </statement>
                    
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement>
                        <p>apply a vertical stretch by a factor of 2 first, followed by a vertical shift 3 units up</p>
                    </statement>
                    
                </choice>

                <choice>
                    <statement>
                        <p>apply a vertical compression by a factor of 2 first, followed by a vertical shift 3 units up</p>
                    </statement>
                    
                </choice>
            </choices>
            
        </exercise>

        <exercise label="multiple-choice-Orderh">
            <title>Multiple-Choice, Randomized, One Answer</title>
            <idx>vert shift first</idx>
            <statement>
                <p>To obtain the graph of <m>h</m>, we</p>
            </statement>
            <choices randomize="yes">
                <choice>
                    <statement>
                        <p>apply a vertical shift 3 down first, followed by a vertical stretch by a factor of 2</p>
                    </statement>
                    
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement>
                        <p>apply a vertical shift 3 up first, followed by a vertical stretch by a factor of 2</p>
                    </statement>
                    
                </choice>
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement>
                        <p>apply a vertical stretch by a factor of 2 first, followed by a vertical shift 3 units up</p>
                    </statement>
                    
                </choice>

                <choice>
                    <statement>
                        <p>apply a vertical compression by a factor of 2 first, followed by a vertical shift 3 units up</p>
                    </statement>
                    
                </choice>
            </choices>
            
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <p>
        It remains to see whether the graph of <m>h</m> or the graph of <m>q</m> corresponds to the correct graph of <m>A\cdot f(x)+k</m>. To see this, we define <m>w(x)=A\cdot f(x)+k</m> in the applet above, and we click on the circle on the left to make its graph visible (in green). The graph will agree with either the graph of <m>h</m> or the graph of <m>q</m>.
    </p>
    <exercises xml:id="exercises-VerticalOrder">
        
        <exercise label="multiple-choice-OrderA2k3">
            <title>Multiple-Choice, Randomized, One Answer</title>
            <idx>vert shift first</idx>
            <statement>
                <p>To obtain the graph of <m>2\cdot f(x)+3</m>, we</p>
            </statement>
            <choices randomize="yes">
                <choice>
                    <statement>
                        <p>apply a vertical shift 3 down first, followed by a vertical stretch by a factor of 2</p>
                    </statement>
                    
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement>
                        <p>apply a vertical shift 3 up first, followed by a vertical stretch by a factor of 2</p>
                    </statement>
                    
                </choice>
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement>
                        <p>apply a vertical stretch by a factor of 2 first, followed by a vertical shift 3 units up</p>
                    </statement>
                    
                </choice>

                <choice>
                    <statement>
                        <p>apply a vertical compression by a factor of 2 first, followed by a vertical shift 3 units up</p>
                    </statement>
                    
                </choice>
            </choices>
            
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <p>
        Before we generalize the observed behavior, let's make sure that if we take negative values for <m>A</m> and <m>k</m>, that behavior still holds. So, set <m>A=-2</m> and <m>k=-3</m>, and repeat the previous steps.
    </p>
     
<exercises xml:id="exercises-VerticalOrder2">
        
        <exercise label="multiple-choice-OrderA-2k-3">
            <title>Multiple-Choice, Randomized, One Answer</title>
            <idx>vert shift first</idx>
            <statement>
                <p>To obtain the graph of <m>-2\cdot f(x)-3</m>, we</p>
            </statement>
            <choices randomize="yes">
                <choice>
                    <statement>
                        <p>apply a vertical shift 3 down first, followed by a vertical stretch by a factor of 2</p>
                    </statement>
                    
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement>
                        <p>apply a vertical shift 3 down first, followed by a vertical compression by a factor of 2</p>
                    </statement>
                    
                </choice>
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement>
                        <p>apply a vertical stretch by a factor of 2 first followed by a reflection across the <m>x-</m>axis, and finally apply a vertical shift 3 units down</p>
                    </statement>
                    
                </choice>

                <choice>
                    <statement>
                        <p>apply a vertical stretch by a factor of 2 first, followed by a vertical shift 3 units down</p>
                    </statement>
                    
                </choice>
            </choices>
            
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <p>
        Now, we generalize what we learned from these examples.
    </p>
    <p>
        <proposition xml:id="prop-VerticalOrder">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>A,k</m> be non-zero real numbers such that <m>g(x)=A\cdot f(x)+k</m>. To obtain the graph of <m>g</m> from the graph of <m>f</m>, apply the vertical transformation in the following order:
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Apply a vertical dilation by a factor of <m>|A|</m>. If <m>A\lt 0</m>, then also apply a reflection across the <m>x-</m>axis.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Apply a vertical shift of <m>k</m> units (up if <m>k\gt 0</m> or down if <m>k\lt 0</m>).
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </proposition>
    </p>
    <exercises xml:id="exercises-VerticalOrderPractice">
        
        <exercise label="multiple-choice-VerticalOrderPoint">
            <title>Multiple-Choice, Randomized, One Answer</title>
            <idx>vert shift first</idx>
            <statement>
                <p>Let <m>g(x)=-4\cdot f(x)+1</m>. If the point <m>(1,-1)</m> lies on the graph of <m>f</m>, then the respective point on the graph of <m>g</m> is</p>
            </statement>
            <choices randomize="yes">
                <choice>
                    <statement>
                        <p><m>(-3,-1)</m></p>
                    </statement>
                    
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement>
                        <p><m>(-4,0)</m></p>
                    </statement>
                    
                </choice>
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement>
                        <p><m>(1,5)</m></p>
                    </statement>
                    
                </choice>

                <choice>
                    <statement>
                        <p><m>(1,0)</m></p>
                    </statement>
                    
                </choice>
            </choices>
            
        </exercise>
    </exercises>
</section>